  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nPersistent upper high remains fixed over the Southern Rockies, \nand will sit there through at least the remainder of late July. \nThis will drive the weather pattern through the week ahead. GFS \n500-mb height anomalies suggest this high is 3-4 standard \ndeviations stronger than is typical for the middle of the North \nAmerican Monsoon. The pattern is not favorable for widespread \nrain, as any storms that do form will be mostly driven by recycled\nmoisture and convective temperature/terrain forcing producing \nmeager instability. The monsoonal plume will be broad and diffuse,\nbut generally favor the AZ/NM state line. Precipitable water \nvalues are just over an inch through much of southwest New Mexico,\nwith drier air further to the east across far west Texas and \nsoutheast New Mexico. \n\nFor Monday, lack of moisture and no forcing aloft will likely \nlead to a \"down\" day of rain chances. Isolated (10-20% coverage) \nshowers and thunderstorms are expected over the high terrain of \nthe Gila Wilderness and NM bootheel. Most other areas will stay \ndry, including nearly all of the Rio Grande Valley. Main threat \nwith any storms will be localized flash flooding, as storm motions\nremain very slow if not stationary and capable of 1-2 inch/hour \nrainfall rates.\n\nThe main headline for the desert lowland will be hot temperatures. \nSuffocating upper ridge aloft will promote very hot afternoon\nconditions, followed by little relief overnight. Highs will run \n8-12 degrees above normal for late July, which means lowland \nhighs in the 102 to 106 degree range through at least next Friday.\nA Heat Advisory is in effect for El Paso and the lower Rio Grande\nValley through Tuesday, and may need to be expanded in both time \nand area later this week.\n\nMinimal changes in the strength or orientation of the high for \nTuesday onward will make it difficult to pinpoint any appreciable \nday-to-day changes with sensible weather elements. Overall \nweather pattern will be warmer-than-normal temperatures and \nisolated rain chances focused mainly across southwest New Mexico\nand the southern NM high elevations. GFS/ECMWF both agree in a \nslight retrograde of the upper high as we head into the first week\nof August, which is still a poor weather pattern for rain \nchances.",
